refs += [dict(id='journal',title='The Determinacy of Context-Free Games — journal version',authors='Olivier Finkel',year=2013,url='https://doi.org/10.2178/jsl.7804050',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/1312.3412',locator='Journal of Symbolic Logic 78(4):1115–1134, December 2013; Cambridge online record dated 12 March 2014. Full author version, Section 5, retains the tree question.'),dict(id='trees',title='On the topological complexity of tree languages',authors='André Arnold; Jacques Duparc; Filip Murlak; Damian Niwiński',year=2008,url='https://www.mimuw.edu.pl/~niwinski/Prace/lobo_d.pdf',locator='Logic and Automata: History and Perspectives, pages 9–28. Author manuscript Section 4, Lemma 4.1: tree-level Wadge game and continuous reduction; Theorem 4.2: Borel case. Some earlier bibliographies date the manuscript 2007.'),dict(id='hierarchy',title='Wadge-Wagner Hierarchy of Regular Tree Languages',authors='Jacques Duparc; Kevin Fournier',year=2016,url='https://www.ims.uni-stuttgart.de/events/TTATT2016/proceedings.pdf',locator='TTATT 2016 workshop proceedings, pages 13–20. Introduction and footnote 1 construct a long hierarchy without a determinacy assumption; this is not a determinacy theorem for all pairs.')]
save(i,dict(title='Determinacy of Wadge games for Muller tree languages',formal=r'Let \(D_{\mathrm{tree}}\) be the statement that, for every pair \(A,B\) of finite nondeterministic Muller automata on infinite full binary labeled trees, the Wadge game \(W(L(A),L(B))\) is determined. Is \(D_{\mathrm{tree}}\) provable in Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory with the axiom of choice, ZFC? The target is provability from these axioms, not an algorithm for finding the winning player.',definitions=r'''For a finite nonempty alphabet \(\Sigma\), a full binary labeled tree is a function \(t:\{0,1\}^*\to\Sigma\). The empty word is the root and the children of node \(u\) are \(u0,u1\). All branches are infinite.

A nondeterministic Muller tree automaton consists of a finite state set \(Q\), initial state \(q_0\), transition relation \(\Delta\subseteq Q\times\Sigma\times Q\times Q\), and an acceptance family \(\mathcal F\subseteq2^Q\). A run assigns a state to every node, starting with \(q_0\), and respects the transition relation at every node and its two children. It is accepting if, on each infinite branch, the set of states occurring infinitely often belongs to \(\mathcal F\). The language \(L(A)\) contains exactly the trees admitting at least one accepting run. No determinism, unambiguity or Borel-language promise is imposed.

In the Wadge game, Player I builds a tree over the alphabet of \(A\), and Player II builds one over the alphabet of \(B\). Both initially have empty finite trees. In each round I appends one or more complete levels, with all their labels, to I's own tree. II sees that move and appends finitely many complete levels to II's tree, or skips. Earlier labels cannot be changed. I therefore produces an infinite tree \(t\). II loses if II's output has only finitely many levels; otherwise it defines an infinite tree \(u\), and II wins exactly when
\[
t\in L(A)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad u\in L(B).
\]
I wins in every other case. II may skip infinitely often, but must also extend the output infinitely often.

This is a perfect-information, length-\(\omega\), zero-sum game. A strategy specifies a legal move after every finite play history at which that player acts. A winning strategy succeeds against every legal opposing play. Strategies are arbitrary set-theoretic functions, with no computability, finite-memory or resource restriction. “Determined” means that one of the two players has a winning strategy.

The tree space has the product topology of discrete node labels: finitely many fixed levels specify basic neighborhoods. The tree-level version is the usual Wadge game; II has a winning strategy exactly when a continuous map \(F\) satisfies \(L(A)=F^{-1}(L(B))\). This equivalence explains the connection to language complexity, but does not assume determinacy.

The single sentence \(D_{\mathrm{tree}}\) universally quantifies over the finite automaton descriptions and asserts the existence of a winning strategy for each pair. The question asks whether ZFC proves that sentence. It does not assert that merely adding some stronger axiom is a negative resolution: such an axiom can prove statements already provable in ZFC.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='A positive answer supplies a ZFC proof of determinacy for every pair in the specified class. A negative answer establishes nonprovability, for example by a relative-consistency or model construction yielding ZFC together with a failure of this universal determinacy statement; any metatheoretic consistency assumption must be stated explicitly. A theorem assuming a stronger determinacy principle without a nonprovability argument, or a proof for a restricted subclass, does not answer the ZFC-provability question.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The tree question differs from the paper’s word results',text='The source proves independence phenomena for Wadge games involving one-counter automata on infinite words. Its conclusion separately leaves the finite-state Muller tree case open; replacing one model with the other would misstate the result.',citation='journal'),dict(title='Borel determinacy covers only a restriction',text='When both tree languages are Borel, determinacy follows from the Borel theorem. Regular infinite-tree languages can be non-Borel, so this observation does not cover arbitrary Muller automata.',citation='trees'),dict(title='Long hierarchies need not settle all games',text='The 2016 workshop paper constructs families of regular tree languages of high Wadge complexity without assuming an additional determinacy principle. Establishing comparisons within such families does not prove that every pair of regular tree languages yields a determined game.',citation='hierarchy')],why='Asks how much set theory is needed to compare properties described by finite automata. A resolution would clarify whether the topological organization of all regular infinite-tree languages rests on ordinary ZFC or needs stronger foundations.',progress=[dict(date='2012',text='The conference conclusion distinguishes the open Muller tree question from the proved counter-automaton word results.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2013-12',text='The journal version retains the same open tree determinacy question.',citation='journal'),dict(date='2016',text='Workshop work constructs long regular-tree Wadge hierarchies without an additional determinacy assumption, but does not establish universal pairwise determinacy.',citation='hierarchy')],references=refs,status='open',status_note='Read the conference and full journal concluding questions, the tree-level game in the authors’ survey, and the scope of the 2016 hierarchy construction. Later-result searches through 12 September 2026 found no resolution of ZFC-provability for all Muller tree language pairs. Word-game independence, Borel determinacy and special hierarchy constructions were not treated as resolutions.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Recovered the missing ZFC-provability target and defined nondeterministic Muller acceptance, complete-level play, skips and unrestricted winning strategies. Kept metatheoretic nonprovability distinct from the truth of determinacy under extra axioms.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A foundational question linking finite automata, infinite games and the axiomatic strength of descriptive language complexity. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['Two players build infinite labeled trees and compare their membership in two regular tree languages.','The second player may delay output but must eventually build a full infinite tree.','The question asks whether ZFC proves that one player always has a winning strategy.','Muller automata may recognize non-Borel languages, so the Borel theorem alone is insufficient.','Known word-game independence and long regular-tree hierarchy constructions do not settle this universal tree question.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','journal','trees','hierarchy'])),notes=['Restored the exact ZFC-provability question rather than an algorithmic winner problem.','Defined full binary trees, nondeterministic Muller runs and branchwise acceptance.','Specified perfect-information level play, legal delays and unrestricted set-theoretic strategies.','Checked the full journal version and later hierarchy work without transferring results from different classes.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.3412'])
